% facility_location: a Picat model from DCP Rosetta.
%
% A company is considering opening warehouses in four cities to meet regional demands at minimal costs. The
% potential cities are New York, Los Angeles, Chicago and Atlanta. Each open warehouse has a fixed weekly cost and
% can ship at most max_shipping units per week, and a closed one ships nothing. Shipping one unit from a warehouse
% to a region has a given cost, and each region must receive at least its weekly demand. The company must decide
% which warehouses to open under these constraints: 1. if New York is opened, then Los Angeles must also be opened;
% 2. no more than three warehouses can be operational in any given week; 3. either Atlanta or Los Angeles must be
% opened. The objective is the total cost: the fixed costs of the open warehouses plus the shipping costs.
%
% Save this file as model.pi. Download `facility_location.json` and `dcp_convert_picat.py` from the
% problem's page:
%   https://dcp-bench.github.io/DCP-Rosetta/problems/facility_location.html
% Convert the instances once, then run the first one (Picat 3.9, https://picat-lang.org):
%   python dcp_convert_picat.py facility_location.json
%   picat model.pi facility_location_1.dat
%
% Data.get(fixed_costs): the fixed weekly cost of each warehouse; Data.get(max_shipping): the most units a warehouse
% ships per week; Data.get(shipping_costs): the cost of shipping one unit from warehouse I to region J (lists
% indexed from 1, warehouses in the order New York, Los Angeles, Chicago, Atlanta); Data.get(demands): the weekly
% demand of each region.
import cp.

model(Data, Vars, Outputs, Options) =>
    FixedCosts = Data.get(fixed_costs),
    MaxShipping = Data.get(max_shipping),
    ShippingCosts = Data.get(shipping_costs),
    Demands = Data.get(demands),
    W = len(FixedCosts),                   % the number of warehouses
    R = len(Demands),                      % the number of regions
    % Open[I] = 1 when warehouse I is opened: 1 New York, 2 Los Angeles, 3 Chicago, 4 Atlanta
    Open = new_list(W),
    Open :: 0..1,
    % Ships[I,J] = the units shipped from warehouse I to region J
    Ships = [new_list(R) : _I in 1..W],
    foreach (I in 1..W, J in 1..R)
        Ships[I,J] :: 0..MaxShipping
    end,
    % if New York is opened, then Los Angeles is opened too
    Open[1] #=< Open[2],
    % no more than three warehouses are operational
    sum(Open) #=< 3,
    % either Atlanta or Los Angeles is opened
    Open[4] + Open[2] #>= 1,
    % a closed warehouse ships nothing, and an open one ships at most max_shipping units
    foreach (I in 1..W)
        sum(Ships[I]) #=< MaxShipping * Open[I]
    end,
    % each region receives at least its weekly demand
    foreach (J in 1..R)
        sum([Ships[I,J] : I in 1..W]) #>= Demands[J]
    end,
    % the total cost: the fixed costs of the open warehouses plus the shipping costs
    Cost #= sum([FixedCosts[I] * Open[I] : I in 1..W]) + sum([ShippingCosts[I,J] * Ships[I,J] : I in 1..W, J in 1..R]),
    Vars = [Open, Ships],
    Outputs = [total_cost = Cost, open_warehouse = Open, ships = Ships],
    % minimise the total cost
    Options = [$min(Cost)].

main([File]) =>
    Fd = open(File),
    Term = read_term(Fd),
    Term = $json_object(Keys, Values),
    close(Fd),
    Data = new_map([K = V : {K, V} in zip(Keys, Values)]),
    model(Data, Vars, Outputs, Options),
    solve(Options, Vars),
    solve(vars(Outputs)),
    foreach(Name = Value in Outputs)
        printf("%w = %w\n", Name, Value)
    end.
